-Source builds carry `patches/postgres@3.4.9.patch` for this behavior. It rejects
-queued and later queries from a disconnected transaction or reserved connection,
-and prevents a released, closed connection from returning to the open pool.
-The patch covers both ESM and CommonJS. The regression suite terminates real
-PostgreSQL backends and checks rejection, pool recovery, and transaction isolation.
-Remove the patch when an upstream release passes these tests. Installs of the
-unmodified `postgres` package outside this workspace do not include the patch.
+The `postgres` dependency is `@steadybrew/postgresjs`, a maintained fork of
+postgres.js installed under the `postgres` name, pinned to an exact version in
+`packages/db/package.json`. It rejects queued and later queries from a
+disconnected transaction or reserved connection, and prevents a released, closed
+connection from returning to the open pool, in both ESM and CommonJS. The
+regression suite terminates real PostgreSQL backends and checks rejection, pool
+recovery, and transaction isolation. Because the dependency itself provides this
+behavior, npm installs of the published packages get it too.
+
+A query on a reserved connection after `release()` rejects with
+`CONNECTION_RELEASED`, also when the connection had already closed, and a query
+on a transaction handle after its transaction ends rejects with
+`TRANSACTION_ENDED`. `CONNECTION_ENDED` still means only that `sql.end()` was
+called. To return to upstream, set the dependency back to `postgres@3.4.9`,
+restore `patches/postgres@3.4.9.patch` with its `patchedDependencies` entries in
+`package.json` and `pnpm-workspace.yaml`, and set the recovery fixture's
+released-connection check back to `CONNECTION_CLOSED`.
 
